#N canvas 20 20 7000 12860 12;
#X declare -path ../../Cut\ It;
#X text 20 20 tempo-bench -- the tempo acceptance run: the clock \, the transport \, the map and the aux LED. STEPPED BY HAND: press GO to run the step that has just been described \, press GO again to describe the next one. Nothing moves until you ask it to \, so the PASS IF is on screen and STILL while you read it. The prompt line always says what the next press will do., f 120;
#X text 20 150 GO ON THE MAC: the bng below \, or the dev panel's ENCODER CLICK -- u_mother-stub sends encbut unconditionally and nothing in Cut It consumes it. TURNING the encoder repeats the current step without advancing \, which is what to do when you looked away., f 120;
#X text 2400 200 ⚠️ GO ON THE DEVICE IS NOT THE ENCODER -- IT IS netcat \, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS NOT OPTIONAL. mother only forwards encbut once a patch has asked with /enableEncoder \, and NOTHING IN Cut It EVER ASKS -- m_organelle leaves the encoder out on purpose. So the click that drives this bench on the Mac is silently dead on the Organelle. Send instead with ./test/run.sh --bench <name> from the Mac. ⚠️ NOT netcat: the line this file used to print does nothing on macOS -- BSD nc exits before the datagram is flushed at -w0 \, and -w1 was measured to fail too \, while the port IS bound and the bench is fine. It looks exactly like a dead bench. The device cannot send to itself either: busybox here has no nc at all. Asking mother to enable the encoder is NOT the fix either: that means writing to oscOut \, and g_oled is its sole owner., f 120;
#X text 20 280 Load it as a THIRD patch after mother.pd and main.pd. It touches nothing in the deployed patch -- it only pushes onto the same buses a controller would. A PASS IF is printed for EVERY step including the ones whose correct result is that nothing happens \, which are otherwise impossible to mark off., f 120;
#X text 2400 20 ON THE MAC EVERY ERROR STEP ALSO PRINTS /sdcard/cut-it-err.cur: write failed \, AND THAT IS NOT A FAILED STEP. u_err keeps its durable log on the Organelle's SD card \, which no Mac has -- so the write is attempted and refused every time. It is in fact the only proof off-device that the logging path ran at all. On the device that line must NOT appear \, and if it does the card is unwritable and the log is being lost silently., f 120;
#X obj 6000 40 r \$0-say;
#X obj 6000 100 print;
#X text 20 360 GO -- click here \, or the dev panel's encoder button. MAC ONLY -- on the device use netcat \, top right., f 60;
#X obj 20 420 bng 40 250 50 0 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -262144 -1 -1;
#X obj 20 500 s \$0-go;
#X obj 300 420 r encbut;
#X obj 300 480 select 1;
#X obj 300 540 t b;
#X obj 300 600 s \$0-go;
#X text 560 480 encbut sends 1 THEN 0 \, so select 1 is what stops one press counting twice., f 46;
#X obj 300 680 netreceive 9998 1;
#X obj 300 740 route go rerun where show;
#X obj 300 800 t b;
#X obj 300 860 s \$0-go;
#X obj 620 800 t b;
#X obj 620 860 s \$0-rerun;
#X text 860 800 rerun fires the CURRENT step again without advancing \, so a visual step can be looked at more than once. [r]epeat in test/run.sh sends it., f 46;
#X obj 1500 800 t b b;
#X obj 1680 860 f;
#X obj 1500 860 f;
#X obj 1500 920 pack f f;
#X obj 1500 980 print bench-at;
#X obj 1500 740 r \$0-set-step;
#X obj 1680 740 r \$0-set-phase;
#X obj 2000 800 t b;
#X obj 2000 860 s \$0-repeat;
#X text 2200 800 show goes through $0-repeat \, which is the encoder's path too -- one way to re-describe a step \, not two., f 46;
#X obj 1400 420 r enc;
#X obj 1400 480 t b;
#X obj 1400 540 s \$0-repeat;
#X text 1660 480 enc is 1 for up and 0 for down -- not plus or minus one -- so either direction repeats., f 46;
#X text 20 920 THE STATE MACHINE. phase 0 means the step has been described and not yet run \; phase 1 means it has run. SETTING THE STEP NUMBER IS WHAT DESCRIBES IT \, so advancing is just an increment -- there is no second path to keep in step., f 120;
#X obj 20 980 r \$0-go;
#X obj 20 1040 f;
#X obj 20 1100 select 0 1;
#X obj 300 1040 r \$0-set-phase;
#X obj 20 1170 t b b;
#X msg 300 1230 1;
#X obj 300 1290 s \$0-set-phase;
#X obj 20 1290 f;
#X obj 20 1350 s \$0-do-run;
#X obj 20 1230 r \$0-rerun;
#X obj 900 1170 t b b;
#X msg 1180 1230 0;
#X obj 1180 1290 s \$0-set-phase;
#X obj 900 1350 f;
#X obj 900 1410 + 1;
#X obj 900 1470 s \$0-set-step;
#X obj 1700 980 r \$0-set-step;
#X obj 1700 1040 t f f f;
#X obj 1700 1110 s \$0-set-inc;
#X obj 1980 1110 s \$0-set-run;
#X obj 1180 1350 r \$0-set-inc;
#X obj 300 1410 r \$0-set-run;
#X obj 2260 1110 s \$0-do-show;
#X obj 2600 980 r \$0-repeat;
#X obj 2600 1040 f;
#X obj 2780 980 r \$0-set-run;
#X obj 2600 1100 s \$0-do-show;
#X obj 3100 980 loadbang;
#X obj 3100 1040 del 500;
#X msg 3100 1100 1;
#X obj 3100 1160 s \$0-set-step;
#X text 20 1660 BEAT COUNTERS. \$0-zero resets one and starts a 10 SECOND window \; the count is latched and printed when that window closes \, and \$0-read reprints the latched value. THE WINDOW IS MACHINE-TIMED ON PURPOSE -- under manual stepping the gap between two steps is however long you take to judge one \, so a count taken between steps would mean nothing. On the Mac with DSP OFF every count reads 0 \, which looks exactly like a dead clock rather than a setting., f 120;
#X obj 20 1860 r clock;
#X obj 20 1930 t b;
#X obj 20 2000 f;
#X obj 180 2060 + 1;
#X obj 180 2120 t f f;
#X obj 420 2180 f;
#X obj 720 1860 r \$0-zero;
#X obj 720 1930 t b b;
#X msg 900 2000 0;
#X obj 720 2060 del 10000;
#X obj 420 2240 t f f;
#X obj 720 2300 f -1;
#X obj 20 2380 print M-BEATS;
#X obj 970 2240 r \$0-read;
#X text 20 2440 latch starts at -1 \, so a count read before its 10 seconds are up says so rather than lying., f 46;
#X obj 1320 1860 c_clock 1 4;
#X obj 1320 1930 t b;
#X obj 1320 2000 f;
#X obj 1480 2060 + 1;
#X obj 1480 2120 t f f;
#X obj 1720 2180 f;
#X obj 2020 1860 r \$0-zero;
#X obj 2020 1930 t b b;
#X msg 2200 2000 0;
#X obj 2020 2060 del 10000;
#X obj 1720 2240 t f f;
#X obj 2020 2300 f -1;
#X obj 1320 2380 print C1-BEATS-ratio-1;
#X obj 2270 2240 r \$0-read;
#X text 1320 2440 latch starts at -1 \, so a count read before its 10 seconds are up says so rather than lying., f 46;
#X obj 2620 1860 c_clock 1.5 4;
#X obj 2620 1930 t b;
#X obj 2620 2000 f;
#X obj 2780 2060 + 1;
#X obj 2780 2120 t f f;
#X obj 3020 2180 f;
#X obj 3320 1860 r \$0-zero;
#X obj 3320 1930 t b b;
#X msg 3500 2000 0;
#X obj 3320 2060 del 10000;
#X obj 3020 2240 t f f;
#X obj 3320 2300 f -1;
#X obj 2620 2380 print C2-BEATS-ratio-1.5;
#X obj 3570 2240 r \$0-read;
#X text 2620 2440 latch starts at -1 \, so a count read before its 10 seconds are up says so rather than lying., f 46;
#X text 20 2580 DESCRIBING A STEP. One select per step rather than one wide fan \, so every cord stays short and local -- the same reason u_map chains its routes., f 120;
#X obj 20 2680 r \$0-do-show;
#X obj 20 2740 select 1;
#X obj 240 2740 t b b b;
#X msg 700 2740 === STEP-01-of-15 === Resting display at 120 BPM;
#X msg 700 2790 PASS IF: Two bars with a small gate mark under each and 120-bpm in the footer. The aux button is lit dark blue.;
#X msg 700 2840 >>> press GO to run step 1 of 15;
#X obj 240 2910 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3000 select 2;
#X obj 240 3000 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3000 === STEP-02-of-15 === Zero the beat counters;
#X msg 700 3050 PASS IF: Nothing changes anywhere. The aux button stays dark blue -- that is the transport and dark blue is stopped. The counters this arms belong to the bench and show up nowhere on the instrument.;
#X msg 700 3100 >>> press GO to run step 2 of 15 -- then WAIT for the printed count before pressing GO again;
#X obj 240 3170 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3260 select 3;
#X obj 240 3260 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3260 === STEP-03-of-15 === Beat counts while stopped;
#X msg 700 3310 PASS IF: These are counted by the bench and printed on this screen -- look at the got line above and not at the instrument. M-BEATS reads 20 or 21 beats. C1-BEATS-ratio-1 reads the same number. C2-BEATS-ratio-1.5 reads 30 or 31 in the same window. C2 over C1 comes out at 1.5 exactly. A zero anywhere is the failure -- on the Mac check DSP is on first.;
#X msg 700 3360 >>> press GO to run step 3 of 15;
#X obj 240 3430 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3520 select 4;
#X obj 240 3520 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3520 === STEP-04-of-15 === Start the transport;
#X msg 700 3570 PASS IF: The aux button turns green and the 404 starts its pattern. Watch EXT on the 404's pattern select screen. The number beside a pad never moves.;
#X msg 700 3620 >>> press GO to run step 4 of 15;
#X obj 240 3690 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3780 select 5;
#X obj 240 3780 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3780 === STEP-05-of-15 === Knob pickup holds;
#X msg 700 3830 PASS IF: ⛔ What you see depends on whether A Save has ever happened. With a saved knobs.txt -- the device after any Storage Save -- the footer does not move. With no knobs.txt -- always on the Mac and on the device after tools/deploy.sh --clean -- the footer reads 10-bpm and the 404's EXT slides down.;
#X msg 700 3880 >>> press GO to run step 5 of 15;
#X obj 240 3950 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 4040 select 6;
#X obj 240 4040 t b b b;
#X msg 700 4040 === STEP-06-of-15 === Knob pickup crosses over;
#X msg 700 4090 PASS IF: The footer reads 500-bpm and the 404's EXT slides up. A slide rather than a snap is correct.;
#X msg 700 4140 >>> press GO to run step 6 of 15;
#X obj 240 4210 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 4300 select 7;
#X obj 240 4300 t b b b;
#X msg 700 4300 === STEP-07-of-15 === Knob tracking after pickup;
#X msg 700 4350 PASS IF: The footer reads 10-bpm on either machine. If it is held here too then the knob is stuck and that is the failure.;
#X msg 700 4400 >>> press GO to run step 7 of 15;
#X obj 240 4470 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 4560 select 8;
#X obj 240 4560 t b b b;
#X msg 700 4560 === STEP-08-of-15 === Tempo above range;
#X msg 700 4610 PASS IF: The footer reads 600-bpm and exactly one alert appears -- a bordered box naming u_tempo. The second 5000 is silent.;
#X msg 700 4660 >>> press GO to run step 8 of 15;
#X obj 240 4730 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 4820 select 9;
#X obj 240 4820 t b b b;
#X msg 700 4820 === STEP-09-of-15 === Tempo below range;
#X msg 700 4870 PASS IF: The footer reads 5-bpm and a second alert appears.;
#X msg 700 4920 >>> press GO to run step 9 of 15;
#X obj 240 4990 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 5080 select 10;
#X obj 240 5080 t b b b;
#X msg 700 5080 === STEP-10-of-15 === Back in range;
#X msg 700 5130 PASS IF: The footer reads 120-bpm and no alert appears at all.;
#X msg 700 5180 >>> press GO to run step 10 of 15;
#X obj 240 5250 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 5340 select 11;
#X obj 240 5340 t b b b;
#X msg 700 5340 === STEP-11-of-15 === Stop the transport;
#X msg 700 5390 PASS IF: The aux button goes dark blue and the 404 stops. But its display must still say EXT. If it falls back to BPM that is the failure.;
#X msg 700 5440 >>> press GO to run step 11 of 15 -- then WAIT for the printed count before pressing GO again;
#X obj 240 5510 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 5600 select 12;
#X obj 240 5600 t b b b;
#X msg 700 5600 === STEP-12-of-15 === Beat counts after stopping;
#X msg 700 5650 PASS IF: M-BEATS is printed above again and reads 20 or 21 beats. The master reference keeps running with the transport stopped -- that is the point of the step. Nothing about it is on the OLED.;
#X msg 700 5700 >>> press GO to run step 12 of 15;
#X obj 240 5770 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 5860 select 13;
#X obj 240 5860 t b b b;
#X msg 700 5860 === STEP-13-of-15 === Panic;
#X msg 700 5910 PASS IF: The aux button turns red and the footer says panic.;
#X msg 700 5960 >>> press GO to run step 13 of 15;
#X obj 240 6030 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 6120 select 14;
#X obj 240 6120 t b b b;
#X msg 700 6120 === STEP-14-of-15 === Aux button transport;
#X msg 700 6170 PASS IF: On the device use the real button. On the Mac use aux-tap and not the aux toggle. First press green and the 404 starts. Second press dark blue and it stops.;
#X msg 700 6220 >>> press GO to run step 14 of 15;
#X obj 240 6290 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 6380 select 15;
#X obj 240 6380 t b b b;
#X msg 700 6380 === STEP-15-of-15 === Knob 1 tempo sweep;
#X msg 700 6430 PASS IF: The row reads bpm and a number. Never og-knob-1 and never a 0-to-1 decimal. While the knob is still held it reads bpm 57 (120) or similar -- the latched tempo first and the knob position in brackets. Once it crosses it reads bpm alone and tracks between 10 and 500 BPM. The 404 follows the sweep.;
#X msg 700 6480 >>> press GO to run step 15 of 15;
#X obj 240 6550 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 6640 t b;
#X msg 240 6640 === BENCH COMPLETE === every step has been run -- reload the patch to go round again;
#X obj 240 6700 s \$0-say;
#X text 20 6780 RUNNING A STEP. The actions fire in the order they are listed -- rightmost trigger outlet first -- and the line saying the step has fired goes out LAST \, off outlet 0 \, so it cannot arrive before the thing it describes., f 120;
